{
  "id": "I-T-007",
  "title": "Split Segments Are Linked NEXT/FIRST",
  "scope": "TRANSITION",
  "authority": "RFC_8984_CALEXT",
  "citations": [
    {
      "doc": "rfc8984.txt",
      "section": "Section 4.1.3",
      "line_start": 767,
      "line_end": 775,
      "verbatim_quote": "If an object is split to make a \"this and future\" change to a recurrence, the original object MUST be truncated to end at the previous occurrence before this split, and a new object is created to represent all the occurrences after the split. A \"next\" relation MUST be set on the original object's \"relatedTo\" property for the UID of the new object. A \"first\" relation for the UID of the first object in the series MUST be set on the new object. Clients can then follow these UIDs to get the complete set of objects if the user wishes to modify them all at once."
    },
    {
      "doc": "rfc8984.txt",
      "section": "Section 1.4.10",
      "line_start": 555,
      "line_end": 559,
      "verbatim_quote": "\"first\": The linked object is the first in a series the linking object is part of. \"next\": The linked object is next in a series the linking object is part of."
    },
    {
      "doc": "draft-ietf-calext-jscalendar-icalendar.txt",
      "section": "Section 2.3.35",
      "line_start": 3632,
      "line_end": 3638,
      "verbatim_quote": "Any RELTYPE parameters convert to the \"relation\" property in that Relation object. The parameter values convert in lowercase."
    }
  ],
  "statement": "For every UID N created by a successful THIS_AND_FUTURE edit of UID U: N has a recurring main component; U's main component carries exactly one RELTYPE=NEXT link and it points to N; no other component links NEXT to N; N carries exactly one RELTYPE=FIRST link, to the first UID of the chain (U's own FIRST target if U had one, else U); and N's NEXT links equal U's NEXT links before the edit (N takes over U's place in the chain).",
  "rationale": "RFC 8984 Section 4.1.3 requires a 'next' relation on the original object for the UID of the new object and a 'first' relation on the new object for the UID of the first object in the series; the Relation definition (Section 1.4.10) gives 'first'/'next' their ordering meaning and the CalExt iCalendar mapping (draft Section 2.3.35) maps them to RELTYPE=FIRST/NEXT. Taking over the original's NEXT when a segment is inserted mid-chain is the store policy that keeps the chain a single ordered list.",
  "check": "split_links_well_formed",
